Let me explain the preflight request mechanism in detail.

What is a Preflight Request?
A preflight request is an HTTP OPTIONS request that the browser automatically sends before certain cross-origin requests to check if the actual request is safe to send. It's like the browser asking the server, "Hey, would you allow this type of request from this origin?" before actually making the real request.

When Preflight Requests Happen
Browsers automatically send preflight requests when:

You're making a cross-origin request (different domain, protocol, or port)
AND one or more of these conditions are true:
You're using an HTTP method other than GET, HEAD, or POST
You're using POST with a content type other than:
application/x-www-form-urlencoded
multipart/form-data
text/plain
You're including custom headers (like Authorization)
Anatomy of a Preflight Request
Here's what a typical preflight request looks like:

CopyInsert
OPTIONS /api/users/guest HTTP/1.1
Host: vab90wx4u0.execute-api.us-west-1.amazonaws.com
Origin: https://sloandev.net
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization
The browser is saying: "I want to make a POST request with Content-Type and Authorization headers from sloandev.net. Is that allowed?"

Expected Server Response
The server should respond with:

